  <h2>Typical drying times by material</h2>
  <p>These are rough ranges for clean water with professional drying equipment started within a day. Gray or black water, long delays, cold or humid weather, and water trapped under other materials all add time.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Typical drying time</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Carpet (pad replaced)</td><td>1 to 3 days</td><td>Pad is usually pulled and replaced; carpet dried in place.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drywall, lightly wet</td><td>2 to 4 days</td><td>Longer behind cabinets, tile or vinyl wallpaper.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wood subfloor and framing</td><td>3 to 7 days</td><td>Moisture must come out of the wood itself, not just the surface.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hardwood flooring</td><td>1 to 2 weeks or more</td><td>Often dried with specialty floor mats; some cupping can relax as it dries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plaster and lath</td><td>1 to 3 weeks</td><td>Dense and slow to release water; common in older homes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Concrete slab or block</td><td>Several weeks</td><td>Basement slabs and block walls hold water deep in the material.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What a professional drying setup does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Extraction</strong> removes standing water and squeezes water out of carpet and pad. Water removed as liquid is far faster than water evaporated.</li>
    <li><strong>Air movers</strong> push air across wet surfaces to speed evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Dehumidifiers</strong> pull that evaporated moisture out of the air so it doesn't settle back into materials.</li>
    <li><strong>Moisture mapping</strong> with meters and thermal cameras shows how far water traveled inside walls and floors, and where to aim equipment.</li>
    <li><strong>Daily readings</strong> track progress, so equipment is moved or removed based on data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What slows drying down</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Waiting. Every hour water sits, it wicks higher into drywall and deeper into wood.</li>
    <li>Layers that trap water: vinyl flooring over a subfloor, tile, cabinets, or several layers of flooring.</li>
    <li>Cold, damp weather or a humid summer, especially in basements.</li>
    <li>Turning equipment off at night to save noise or power.</li>
    <li>Contaminated water, which means removal and cleaning before drying can even start.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to tell it's actually dry</h2>
  <p>Professionals measure moisture in the wet materials and compare it with readings from the same material in an unaffected room of the same house. That reference is the "dry standard." Equipment should come out when wet areas reach it, and the company should be able to show you the readings. Ask for the drying log if your insurer is paying; it documents that the job was done properly.</p>

  <h2>When drying turns into removal</h2>
  <p>Some materials can't be dried economically or safely. Carpet pad, insulation, particleboard, laminate and drywall that's swollen, crumbling or touched by sewage are usually removed. If drying hasn't started within a couple of days and there's a musty smell or visible growth, treat it as a mold problem as well as a drying job.</p>
